    "abstract_tex": "\nFor the three-dimensional incompressible Navier--Stokes equations, the\nvorticity stretching and transport terms can be compressed into the curl of\nthe solenoidal Lamb vector\n\\(L=\\Pp(u\\times\\omega)\\).  This paper studies a signed Littlewood--Paley\nshell injection built from that representation.  The associated vertical\nheat extension has an unconditional bulk estimate: after normalization by\nenstrophy, its full heat-height integral belongs to \\(L^1\\) in physical time\nfor every periodic Leray--Hopf solution on a finite interval.  The estimate\npersists under bounded-overlap spatial localization and yields a conditional\nlocal continuation criterion.\n\nThe bulk estimate does not recover the bottom trace.  A fixed smooth radial\ntight frame and an explicit global smooth 2D3C Navier--Stokes family along\ndyadic frequencies \\(K=2^j\\) give an exact bottom-to-bulk factor \\(2K^2\\).\nThe same family shows that matched\npartitions do not hide the loss, while a covariant interior-cylinder\nconstruction rules out every geometry-only factor \\(o(r^{-2})\\).  A second\nconstruction uses the finite-dimensional implicit-function theorem for the\nexact Navier--Stokes flow to prescribe a positive-time full-shell zero with\nnonzero nonlinear forcing.  After an amplitude-frequency scaling, its\nscale-invariant entry atom is of order \\(\\lambda^{-4}\\), whereas the bare\nnormalized Leray--Lamb time budget is of order \\(\\lambda^{-6}\\).  Thus no\nuniform bare payment of such internal entries is possible.  An outgoing\ncoarea identity gives an exact scale-matched representation of every\nfinite-order entry, but its zero-level occupation density is not controlled\nhere.  None of the results proves global regularity or constructs a\nsingularity.  Computer-assisted finite Fourier, spectral-reconstruction, and\nGalerkin calculations are used as audits and corroboration; no analytic\ntheorem is inferred from floating-point output.\n",
